      "id": "acc73bdfb939-q1",
      "number": 1,
      "question": "Statement: Should the admission to professional courses in India be given only on merit without any concession to any particular group of students? Arguments: I. Yes, this will improve the quality of the professionals as they will be able to complete the courses successfully. II. No, this will keep large number of socially and economically backward students out of the reach of the professional courses.",
      "questionHtml": "<p>Statement:</p><p>Should the admission to professional courses in India be given only on merit without any concession to any particular group of students?</p><p>Arguments:</p><p>I. Yes, this will improve the quality of the professionals as they will be able to complete the courses successfully.</p><p>II. No, this will keep large number of socially and economically backward students out of the reach of the professional courses.</p>",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "",
          "html": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "",
          "html": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "",
          "html": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "",
          "html": ""
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "4",
      "solution": "<p>In this case, we are accepting argument II as the strong one, and argument I as not strong.</p><p>Argument II is strong because it raises a valid concern about the potential consequences of admitting students solely based on merit. It points out that such a policy might lead to the exclusion of socially and economically backward students. This argument emphasizes the importance of providing opportunities for these disadvantaged groups to access professional courses.</p><p>Argument I, on the other hand, is considered not strong in this scenario because it argues in favor of admitting students based solely on merit without considering the potential consequences for socially and economically backward students. While it promotes the idea of academic quality, it doesn't address the issue of social equity.</p><p>So, if only argument II is correct, the answer is d Only argument II is strong.</p>",
